.TH PVE\-SMART\-ALERT 8 "2026-10-17" "pve\-smart\-alert" "System Administration"
.nh
.SH NAME
pve\-smart\-alert \- SMART drive alerts for Proxmox VE
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pve\-smart\-alert
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B pve\-smart\-alert check
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert scan
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert doctor
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert config check
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert config show
.RB [ \-\-defaults ]
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert test\-notification
.RB [ \-\-severity
.IR severity ]
.RB [ \-\-transport
.IR transport ]
.br
.B pve\-smart\-alert dump\-fixture
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR directory ]
.RB { \-\-all | \fIdevice\fR\ ...}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pve\-smart\-alert
reads the SMART data of every drive of a Proxmox VE host with
.BR smartctl (8),
evaluates it against a catalog of checks, and reports problems through the
notification system of Proxmox VE, with mail to root as the fallback.
A problem is reported once, again when it gets worse, as a reminder after a
while, and when it has cleared.
.PP
The package runs
.B pve\-smart\-alert check
from the systemd timer
.BR \%pve\-smart\-alert.timer ,
3 minutes after boot and then 30 minutes after the start of the previous
run.
The tool only reads from drives; it never enables SMART, starts a self\-test
or changes a setting of a drive, and it changes no file on the host except
its own.
smartctl itself creates the device nodes it needs for some RAID controllers.
.PP
Run the commands as root: the state, the drives and the notification
configuration are readable by root only.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Run one check: find and read the drives, evaluate them, compare the result
with the state of earlier runs, send the notifications that are due and save
the state.
The first run sends one summary of the drives it monitors.
Each notification is logged as one line, and the run as a summary line.
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
print the findings and the notifications a run would send now, and send and
change nothing.
.TP
.B scan
Find the drives of the host and show every device found with its source
(the device scan of smartctl, the probe for HPE Smart Array controllers, or a
manual entry) and what became of it: monitored, in standby, unreadable, or
skipped with a reason.
A drive that two sources find is read twice and kept once; a device that is
listed twice with the same name and type is read once.
For a drive it shows model, serial number, class, what uses it, its wear
value with the source and, for a drive read as
.BR megaraid,N ,
.BR sat+megaraid,N ,
.BR scsi+megaraid,N ,
.BR cciss,N ,
.BR sat+cciss,N
or
.BR scsi+cciss,N ,
the controller and the device ID; any other drive shows the node and type
instead.
A model or serial number that holds a character the tool does not print, or
that is longer than 200 characters, is shown cleaned and cut, with
.B …
for a cut value; the line
.B Selector:
then carries the value as a TOML string, as it is written into
.BR [[drive]] .
A value longer than 200 characters is cut in that line as well and carries
.BR … ;
such a drive is selected with a model pattern.
A value with a lone surrogate, which TOML cannot write, gets the line
.B Cannot select:
with the model pattern instead.
A controller whose drives smartctl cannot read is named above the devices.
The output holds the real serial numbers and WWNs.
.TP
.B status
Show, from the stored state, the last run and every drive with its active
conditions.
Nothing is read from a drive.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the state as JSON.
.TP
.B doctor
Report one line for each part of the host the tool works with:
.BR config ,
.BR smartctl ,
.BR bridge ,
.BR templates ,
.BR mail ,
.BR routing ,
.BR fallback ,
.BR state ,
.BR timer ,
.BR pve ,
.BR smartd ,
.B controllers
and
.BR probe .
Each line is
.BR ok ,
.BR warn ,
.B fail
or
.BR skip ,
with advice where something can be done.
.TP
.B config check
Validate the configuration file and report its first error, with its key
where it has one and its line where it can be found.
A file that passes is saved as the last good configuration.
.TP
.B config show
Print every key with its effective value and where the value comes from, the
file, the built\-in default or the last good configuration.
While the configuration file cannot be used and a last good configuration
exists, each value that carries an origin is marked as the last good
configuration and the header names its file. Without a last good
configuration the built\-in defaults apply. While the last good
configuration is in use, it is the source of the whole report, including
the built\-in defaults it fills in.
With
.BR \-\-defaults ,
print the built\-in defaults.
.TP
.B test\-notification
Send one test notification and report what the notification system did with
it, and which matchers route it.
.B \-\-severity
is one of
.BR info " (the default), " notice ", " warning " and " error .
.B \-\-transport
is one of
.BR auto ", " pve\-notify " and " mail ,
and replaces the configured transport for this test.
.TP
.B dump\-fixture
Record the device scan, lsblk and the output of smartctl for every device
of the scan and of the manual entries
.RB ( \-\-all )
or for the named devices, as one archive in the current directory or in
.BR \-\-output .
A named device has to be one that the scan or a manual entry lists; for any
other name nothing is written.
The drives that only the HPE probe finds are not recorded; add them as manual
entries of the form
.B cciss,N
first.
Serial numbers, WWNs and UUIDs are replaced by placeholders; nothing is
written when a real value would be left.
Model names, firmware versions, the node name and the labels of pools and
volume groups are kept: look at the archive before you share it.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is
.IR \%/etc/pve\-smart\-alert/config.toml ,
in TOML.
Every key is optional; without the file the defaults apply.
.I \%/usr/share/doc/pve\-smart\-alert/config.reference.toml
lists every key with its default and explains it.
A file that does not validate never stops a run: the last configuration that
passed is used, or the defaults, and the problem is reported as
.BR \%tool.config_invalid .
.PP
The sections are
.B [smartctl]
(path, timeout and detail level),
.B \%[discovery]
(exclude rules, the HPE probe and manual devices in
.BR \%[[discovery.device]] ),
.B [notify]
and
.B \%[notify.reminder]
(transport and notification policy),
.B [checks."\fIcheck\-id\fB"]
(the thresholds and confirmation count that the check has, its enabled
state, and the enabled state of a condition of the tool), and
.B [[drive]]
(overrides for one drive, selected by model pattern, serial number or WWN).
.PP
The interval of the timer is not a key of this file.
Change it with a drop\-in, written by
.BR "systemctl edit \%pve\-smart\-alert.timer" .
The timer settings of a drop\-in add to those of the package
.RB ( systemd.timer (5)),
so the drop\-in first empties
.BR OnUnitActiveSec= ,
which resets
.B OnBootSec=
as well, and then sets both:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Timer]
OnUnitActiveSec=
OnBootSec=3min
OnUnitActiveSec=1h
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed.
For
.BR check ,
whatever the health of the drives.
.TP
.B 1
A problem:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR check :
a notification could not be handed over and is planned again on the next
run, or the run failed;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "check \-\-dry\-run" :
the drives have findings;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scan :
the device scan failed;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR status :
the state file cannot be read;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR doctor :
a line failed;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "config check" :
the file is invalid;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR test\-notification :
the test was not delivered;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR dump\-fixture :
nothing was written.
.RE
.TP
.B 2
Wrong use of the command line; and for
.BR scan ,
.B test\-notification
and
.BR dump\-fixture ,
an invalid configuration file, while
.BR check ,
.B doctor
and
.B config show
carry on with the last good configuration.
.TP
.B 3
For
.BR check ,
another run holds the lock; the service counts this as success.
.TP
.B 130
The command was interrupted with Ctrl+C.
It says so on standard error instead of ending in a traceback.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/etc/pve\-smart\-alert/config.toml
The configuration.
.TP
.I \%/var/lib/pve\-smart\-alert/state.json
The state: what was reported and when.
Deleting it makes the next run a first run.
.TP
.I \%/var/lib/pve\-smart\-alert/state.json.corrupt\-\fItimestamp\fP
A state file that could not be used, set aside by the run that found it.
.TP
.I \%/var/lib/pve\-smart\-alert/last\-good\-config.json
The last configuration that passed validation.
.TP
.I \%/run/pve\-smart\-alert/
The lock of a run and the stamp of the failure mail.
.TP
.I \%/usr/libexec/pve\-smart\-alert/notify\-bridge
The program that hands a notification to the notification system of
Proxmox VE.
.TP
.I \%/usr/libexec/pve\-smart\-alert/report\-failure
The failure reporter, started through
.B OnFailure=
when a run of
.B \%pve\-smart\-alert.service
fails; it mails the last 30 lines of the run to root, at most once a day
and once more after a reboot. When the failed invocation wrote no journal
line, as with a failing
.BR ExecStartPre ,
the mail says that the last lines are not available.
.TP
.I \%/usr/lib/systemd/system/pve\-smart\-alert.timer
.TQ
.I \%/usr/lib/systemd/system/pve\-smart\-alert.service
.TQ
.I \%/usr/lib/systemd/system/pve\-smart\-alert\-failure.service
The timer, the service that runs a check, and the unit that reports a failed
run.
.TP
.I \%/usr/share/pve\-manager/templates/default/pve\-smart\-alert\-*.txt.hbs
The two templates of the notifications of the tool.
.TP
.I \%/usr/share/doc/pve\-smart\-alert/config.reference.toml
The reference configuration.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
Every notification has the type
.B pve\-smart\-alert
and the fields
.BR type ,
.B hostname
and
.BR event .
One about a single drive also carries
.B device
and, where the drive reports them,
.B serial
and
.BR model ;
one about the tool itself carries
.B device
with the value
.BR pve\-smart\-alert .
A drive read as
.BR megaraid,N ,
.BR sat+megaraid,N ,
.BR scsi+megaraid,N ,
.BR cciss,N ,
.BR sat+cciss,N
or
.BR scsi+cciss,N
also carries
.BR device\-id ;
a drive behind another controller carries no such field.
A matcher with
.B \%match\-field regex:type=^pve\-smart\-alert$
selects all of them.
A notification that goes as mail to root, with
.B transport = "mail"
or as the fallback of
.BR auto ,
reaches the notification system as
.B system\-mail
with the severity
.B unknown
and only the fields
.B type
(with the value
.BR system\-mail )
and
.BR hostname .
.B pve\-smart\-alert doctor
warns when a severity of the tool reaches no enabled target.
